The safety of believing in someone looking out for you. And believing that death is not the end. Some may agree with religious beliefs' comfort. But others might question Pascal's reasoning. Does seeking solace solely based on logic truly earn one a ticket to heaven? Doesn't God desire self-sacrifice instead? Not according to Pascal.  He believes. How and why you choose to believe doesn’t matter. The key is, he thinks. God cares not how he gets the devotee; 
he simply needs them. So what dictates your path to embracing a belief system? Easy! Pascal suggests employing self-programming into true belief. This would allow initially self-interested motivations to evolve into honest conviction.   This happens by actively taking action, such as attending church services or regularly praying while socializing with other believers. It may appear odd and disingenuous at the initial stages but over time, it becomes a fundamental component of your belief system. Think about  Indiana Jones' movie series: Indiana Jones and the Last Crusade. This film is considered pop culture's best allegory for pragmatic conviction by action. Throughout all "Indiana Jones" movies I am only pretending that the fourth one was never made; Indy’s character is depicted as a skeptical figure; 
he hunts religious relics. The power of those relics is just “hocuses pocus” to him.  But at the culmination of  “The Last Crusade,” in ancient temples, Indy locates the Holy Grail after successfully navigating a series of booby-traps and proving his faith. However, this pursuit is not because he randomly embraces God but rather, he acts through his beliefs on the quest for him—he does it for the sake of finding the grail,
not just faith itself—because this means finding the grail and winning the treasure. We should remember; Indy literally goes through a path that proves the worth of those who believe in the world or what might lie ahead - faith-based action. What makes religion, according to Kierkegaard. 


To sum it up, religion is about risking something for no apparent benefit other than believing—leaping off the precipice, like the case of India Jones; jumping and hoping that God catches you. As a reminder: we cannot see this until the end of our program. After all, we have to give up on reason if we want to discover truth – and belief alone does not provide us with evidence.
